<swi_base name="Options">
<swi number="&amp;52F80" name="OpenFile" description="Open an options file">
<swi_in>
 <swi_reg r=0>Flags :
              <bittable>
               <bit number="0-1">Method of accessing file
                <valuetable>
                <value number=0>R1 contains absolute filename</value>
                <value number=1>R1 contains the leaf name<br>
                                R2 contains the application name,<br>
                                R3 contains another directory name to use
                                if Choices not present, or Choices file not
                                present.</value>
                <value number="2-3">Reserved</value>
                </valuetable>
               </bit>
               <bit number="2-31">Reserved, must be 0</bit>
              </bittable>
              </swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=1>Pointer to filename, or leafname</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=2>Pointer to application name</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=3>Pointer to directory to use</swi_reg>
</swi_in>
<swi_out>
 <swi_reg r=0>Opaque options handle</swi_reg>
</swi_out>

<swi_use>
This SWI is used to open a specific, or Choices: file for access. When
opening a specific file (type=0), only that file will be written to and read
from.

<p>When opening a Choices: file (type=1), the file read will come from
<code>Choices:&lt;application&gt;.&lt;leafname&gt;</code> if that is present,
or if not, from <code>&lt;directory&gt;.&lt;leafname&gt;</code>. This
provides compatibility for applications written for older systems. When
writing, data will be written to
<code>&lt;Choices$Write&gt;.&lt;application&gt;.&lt;leafname&gt;</code>
unless the Choices: structure is not present, in which case it will be
written to <code>&lt;directory&gt;.&lt;leafname&gt;</code>.
</swi_use>
</swi>

<swi number="&amp;52F81" name="CloseFile" description="Close a file, writing out any changes that are pending">
<swi_in>
 <swi_reg r=0>Opaque file handle</swi_reg>
</swi_in>

<swi_use>
This SWI is used to close an Options file session. Any changes are written
out to the relevant file. An error may be produced if the options file could
not be written.
</swi_use>
</swi>

<swi number="&amp;52F82" name="ReadGroup" description="Reads in a group description block to produce a value/tag array">
<swi_in>
 <swi_reg r=0>Opaque file handle</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=1>Pointer to group name</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=2>Flags :
              <bittable>
               <bit number="0-7">Group type :
                                 <valuetable>
                                  <value number="0">Options format</value>
                                  <value number="1">List format</value>
                                  <value number="other">Reserved</value>
                                 </valuetable>
                                 </bit>
               <bit number="8-31">Reserved, must be 0</bit>
              </bittable>
              </swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=3>Pointer to buffer, or 0 to return size of buffer required</swi_reg>
</swi_in>

<swi_out>
 <swi_reg r=0>Buffer size required, if r3 = 0 on entry</swi_reg>
</swi_out>

<swi_use>
<P>This SWI is used to read a group structure into a block of memory. The block
will be in one of two formats.
<P>For an options group the format it:
<offsettable>
 <offset number=0 length=4>Pointer to tag 0</offset>
 <offset number=4 length=4>Pointer to value 0</offset>
 <offsetnote>Repeated until...</offsetnote>
 <offset number="x" length=4>0</offset>
 <offset number="x+4" length=4>0</offset>
</offsettable>
For a list format group the buffer is filled with a series of 0 terminated strings
immediately following each other.
</swi_use>
</swi>

<swi number="&amp;52F83" name="WriteGroup" description="Writes a group description block to the options file">
<swi_in>
 <swi_reg r=0>Opaque file handle</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=1>Pointer to group name</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=2>Flags :
              <bittable>
               <bit number="0-7">Group type :
                                 <valuetable>
                                  <value number="0">Options format</value>
                                  <value number="1">List format</value>
                                  <value number="other">Reserved</value>
                                 </valuetable>
                                 </bit>
               <bit number="8-31">Reserved</bit>
              </bittable>
              </swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=3>Pointer to buffer</swi_reg>
</swi_in>

<swi_use>
This SWI is used to write data from a descriptions block to a file.
The contents of the buffer should be in the same format as for Options_ReadGroup.
</swi_use>
</swi>

<swi number="&amp;52F84" name="ReadValue" description="Reads the value of a single options tag">
<swi_in>
 <swi_reg r=0>Opaque file handle</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=1>Pointer to group name</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=2>Pointer to tag name</swi_reg>
</swi_in>

<swi_out>
 <swi_reg r=0>Pointer to a 0 terminated string containing the value for the tag</swi_reg>
</swi_out>

<swi_use>
This SWI is used to read the value of a single option. The returned string should be
considered read only.
</swi_use>
</swi>

<swi number="&amp;52F85" name="WriteValue" description="Writes the value of a single options tag">
<swi_in>
 <swi_reg r=0>Opaque file handle</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=1>Pointer to group name</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=2>Pointer to tag name</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=3>Pointer to tag value</swi_reg>
</swi_in>

<swi_use>
This SWI is used to write the value of a single option.
</swi_use>
</swi>

<swi number="&amp;52F86" name="ReadBool" description="Converts a string into a boolean value">
<swi_in>
 <swi_reg r=0>Pointer to a string</swi_reg>
</swi_in>

<swi_out>
 <swi_reg r=0>0 or 1 depending on the contents of the string</swi_reg>
</swi_out>

<swi_use>
<p>This SWI will convert a string into a boolean value.
<p>The strings are converted as follows, ignoring case:
 <valuetable name="String" number="Boolean value">
  <value number=true>TRUE (1)</value>
  <value number=yes>TRUE (1)</value>
  <value number=on>TRUE (1)</value>
  <value number=1>TRUE (1)</value>
  <value number=0>FALSE (0)</value>
  <value number=no>FALSE (0)</value>
  <value number=off>FALSE (0)</value>
  <value number=false>FALSE (0)</value>
 </valuetable>
</swi_use>
</swi>

<swi number="&amp;52F86" name="ReadEnum" description="Converts a string into a integer value">
<swi_in>
 <swi_reg r=0>Pointer to a string</swi_reg>
 <swi_reg r=1>Pointer to an array of strings</swi_reg>
</swi_in>

<swi_out>
 <swi_reg r=0>The index into the array that matches the given string.
              Or -1 if it doesn't match any of them.</swi_reg>
</swi_out>

<swi_use>
<p>This SWI will convert a string into a index value.
<p>The string array is a series of 0 terminated strings immediately following
each other, terminated by a single 0 byte.

</swi_use>
</swi>

<!-- Data formats produced, or used by this document -->
<section name="Data formats" tag="formats">

<P>The options files are standard RISC OS text files. Any whitespace (space
or tab) at the beginning or end of a line will be ignored. Any line where the
first non-whitespace character is '#' is a comment line and will be ignored
as will any lines consisting of only whitespace.

<P>The file consists of a series of groups delimited by group name
specifiers. A group name specifier starts with '[' followed by the name,
which may contain spaces, followed by ']'.

<P>The groups themselves are in one of two format (although further formats
may be added in future). The first format (list format) is a simple list of
newline terminated strings.

<p>The second, more complex format consists of tag, value pairs in the
format:

<P><code>&lt;spaces&gt;tag name&lt;spaces&gt;:&lt;spaces&gt;value&lt;spaces&gt;\n</code>
<P>Where <code>&lt;spaces&gt;</code> is 0 or more space or tabs.
<P>An example options file would be:
<pre>
# choices file for widget

[auto load]
# a list of files to load when starting widget
ADFS::4.$.afile
ADFS::4.$.somedir.anotherfile
ADFS::4.$.thingy

[misc options]
somevalue    : 42
window width : 332
auto open    : Yes
</pre>

