<!-- How this document will be tested for compliance -->
<section name="Testing scheme" tag="testing">
<!-- Meeting this means that we have succeeded -->
<subsection name="Acceptance test" tag="acceptance">
<p>The options file parser must be capable of coping with very malformed
files. We have to expect that users (and programmers) will edit the files by
hand and often get that wrong. At worse the program should fail to read a few
options, but even in the worst cases the vast majority of the file should be
able to be read.

<p>The layout of option files must be preserved as it will be layed out in
such a way as to make it easier for the human reader. So, any whitspace must
remain in the file after it is modified. As should any comments. If no
changes are made to the options then the file written out should be identical
to the file read in.

<p>Transition to the Options system should be as painless as possible for
both programmers and users.

<!-- How we will perform tests for compliance to specification -->
<subsection name="Test strategy" tag="teststrategy">
The Options module should be tested by a number of programmers, in their own
applications, as well as in some of the OS components.

